# kills: a card in flight never widens the page, at 380x800 and 844x390
# suite: node --test --test-name-pattern ^card.swipe:.a.card.in.flight.never.widens.the.page,.at.380x800.and.844x390$ tests/e2e.test.js
# Drops `main{overflow-x:clip}`: a card mid fly-out (translated well past the
# viewport edge) now widens the page's scrollable area instead of being
# clipped, at both a portrait and a landscape viewport.
# Context-refreshed 2026-09-30 (S2): main/.scene's touch-action moved from
# "pan-y pinch-zoom" to "none" and the comment above gained the iOS
# swipe-only rationale - the mutation itself (dropping overflow-x:clip) is
# unchanged.
# The test itself needed a fix alongside this refresh: at the 380x800 mobile
# viewport, once overflow-x:clip is gone the overflowing content widens
# Chrome's mobile LAYOUT viewport itself (the classic "horizontal scroll on
# mobile" failure), so window.innerWidth grows right along with
# document.documentElement.scrollWidth and a scrollWidth<=innerWidth check
# passed trivially even with this mutation applied. The test now compares
# scrollWidth against the fixed viewport width passed to setViewport()
# instead, which this mutation correctly fails (478 > 380).
diff --git a/index.html b/index.html
--- a/index.html
+++ b/index.html
@@ -430,7 +430,6 @@ main{flex:1; display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; width:100
    zoom the page, so both main and .scene drop to touch-action:none - main
    for the same mid-flight-hit-test reason as above. */
 main{touch-action:none}
 .scene{touch-action:none}
-main{overflow-x:clip}
 /* The card is sized from whichever axis runs out first.
    --card-w caps the WIDTH (88vw, 46vh, 420px), but taking it as the width and
    letting aspect-ratio derive the height is a one-way constraint, and in
